---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// The address people use when something has gone wrong at a table.
const CONTACT = 'diceandtale@gmail.com';
---
<Base
  title="Code of Conduct"
  description="What Dice & Tale expects of everyone at the table, and what to do if something goes wrong."
>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Non-negotiable</p>
      <h1>At the table</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Roleplaying puts strangers in a room and asks them to be vulnerable in front of each
        other. That only works if everybody is safe.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule--double" />

  <section>
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow prose">
      <h2>What we expect</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Everyone at the table is a guest.</strong> Harassment, slurs, and hostility
          based on who somebody is are not tolerated, in the fiction or out of it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Consent is ongoing.</strong> Content warnings are published for each table.
          If a scene is heading somewhere a player does not want to go, it stops. No
          explanation is required and none should be asked for.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Safety tools are normal, not a failure.</strong> Every table runs lines and
          veils, and any player can call a scene back at any moment. Using one is not
          disruptive; ignoring one is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The game master runs the table, and is also a guest.</strong> They can ask a
          player to leave a table. They are also held to everything on this page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Do not turn a table into a spotlight.</strong> Four hours divided by five
          players is not much time each. Watch how much of it you are taking.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Adult content stays on adult tables.</strong> Tables marked 18+ are marked
          for a reason, and the rest are not.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The tools every table uses</h2>
      <p>
        These are not exceptional measures. They run at every table, every time,
        the same way a seatbelt is not an accusation.
      </p>
      <dl class="tools">
        <dt>Content warnings</dt>
        <dd>
          Each table publishes what it cannot avoid — roughly three things, the
          core of the story rather than an exhaustive list. They are on the table
          listing before you book, not sprung on you at the table.
        </dd>

        <dt>Lines and veils</dt>
        <dd>
          In the first few minutes the table agrees its <em>lines</em> — things that
          will not appear at all — and its <em>veils</em> — things that can happen
          but off-screen, described rather than played. Anybody can name one. Nobody
          has to say why.
        </dd>

        <dt>Stopping a scene</dt>
        <dd>
          Any player, at any moment, can say stop, or tap the table, or slide the
          card. The scene rewinds or cuts away and play carries on. It is not a
          complaint, it is not a scene about you, and no explanation is asked for.
        </dd>

        <dt>Session zero, compressed</dt>
        <dd>
          A one-shot doesn't get a whole session of setup, so the game master does
          the short version at the start: what the game is about, what it will not
          do, and how to stop it.
        </dd>
      </dl>

      <h2>If something goes wrong</h2>
      <p>
        Tell a game master, or tell whoever is running the door. You can also write to
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a> afterwards, and you can do that
        anonymously if you would rather.
      </p>
      <p>
        We will listen to you before we do anything, we will not make you confront anybody,
        and we will not tell the person who reported something to somebody else. Depending on
        what happened, the outcome can be a conversation, a table change, or being asked to
        leave and not come back.
      </p>

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p class="short">
        Come to play. Make room for the people next to you. If somebody says stop, stop.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose h2 { margin: 2.4rem 0 0.9rem; }
  .prose h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .prose ul { padding-left: 1.15rem; }
  .prose li { margin-bottom: 0.85rem; max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose li::marker { color: var(--gold); }
  .tools { margin: 1.4rem 0 0; }
  .tools dt {
    font-family: "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--gold); margin: 1.5rem 0 0.45rem;
  }
  .tools dd { margin: 0; max-width: 64ch; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .short {
    font-family: "Cinzel", Georgia, serif; font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.5;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--gold); padding-left: 1.2rem; margin-top: 1.2rem;
  }
</style>
